%I A398775 #22 Nov 11 2026 20:51:39
%S A398775 1,1,5,48,1,30,85,935,528,2505,840,1190,29382,25176,12685,67852,
%T A398775 299549,62406,860796
%N A398775 a(n) is the smallest positive integer that makes p(n) = (2 * a(n) * p(n-1))^2 + 1 a prime with p(0) = 1.
%C A398775 Recursively, all p(n) are generalized Fermat primes in the form p(n) = (2 * a(n) * p(n-1))^2 + 1.
%C A398775 p(19), a 608074 digits prime, was discovered by Peimao Jiang.
%H A398775 PrimePages, <a href="https://t5k.org/primes/page.php?id=96548">p(18)</a>.
%H A398775 Lei Zhou, <a href="https://csic.som.emory.edu/~lzhou/blogs/?p=187">Recursive Generalized Fermat Prime found</a>.
%e A398775 From p(0) = 1, the smallest a(1) is 1 such that p(1) = (2 * a(1) * p(0))^2 + 1 = (2 * 1 * 1)^2 + 1 = 5 is prime.
%e A398775 Keep going, p(2) = (2 * a(2) * p(1))^2 + 1 = (2 * 1 * 5)^2 + 1 = 101 is prime, such that a(2) = 1.
%e A398775 Keep going, p(3) = (2 * a(3) * p(2))^2 + 1 = (2 * 5 * 101)^2 + 1 = 1020101 is prime, such that a(3) = 5.
%t A398775 p = 1; Table[n = 0; While[n++; cp = (2*n*p)^2 + 1; ! PrimeQ[cp]]; p = cp; n, {i, 1, 11}]
%Y A398775 Cf. A002496, A121326, A168024, A163588.
%K A398775 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A398775 1,3
%A A398775 _Lei Zhou_, Nov 03 2026
